1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,520
Double your speed and your braking

2
00:00:01,520 --> 00:00:03,040
distance doesn't double.

3
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:04,640
It quadruples.

4
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:07,120
Fourteen meters becomes fifty five.

5
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:09,680
Here's the one equation behind it, and why

6
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:12,080
every road engineer knows it by heart.

7
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:13,920
Let's work it out with nothing more than

8
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:14,880
algebra.

9
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:17,120
When you hit the brakes, the car slows

10
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:19,120
down at a roughly constant rate.

11
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:21,120
Call that deceleration a.

12
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:23,360
On a dry road it's about seven meters per

13
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:25,280
second, every second.

14
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:27,440
The car starts at speed v and ends at

15
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:30,160
zero. One of the basic equations of motion

16
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:32,080
connects those, and solving it for the

17
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:34,150
distance gives d equals v squared over two

18
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:35,040
a.

19
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:37,200
That little square is everything.

20
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:40,000
Double v, and v squared becomes four times

21
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,880
bigger. At fifty kilometers per hour,

22
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:45,120
about fourteen meters per second, the car

23
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:47,360
brakes in about fourteen meters.

24
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:49,830
At one hundred kilometers per hour, about

25
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:52,160
twenty eight meters per second, it needs

26
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:54,000
about fifty five meters.

27
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,440
Four times as far.

28
00:00:55,770 --> 00:00:57,530
Here's the whole picture.

29
00:00:57,530 --> 00:01:00,250
Braking distance grows along a parabola.

30
00:01:00,250 --> 00:01:02,650
Every extra ten kilometers per hour costs

31
00:01:02,650 --> 00:01:04,570
more distance than the ten before it, so

32
00:01:04,570 --> 00:01:06,890
the curve gets steeper the faster you go.

33
00:01:06,890 --> 00:01:09,450
Engineers use this one line everywhere.

34
00:01:09,450 --> 00:01:12,170
It helps set speed limits near schools.

35
00:01:12,170 --> 00:01:14,250
It decides how far a driver must be able

36
00:01:14,250 --> 00:01:16,490
to see before a curve or a junction.

37
00:01:16,490 --> 00:01:18,410
And it tells car designers how much

38
00:01:18,410 --> 00:01:21,450
braking power a faster, heavier car needs.

39
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:23,210
One honest detail.

40
00:01:23,210 --> 00:01:25,530
Before you even touch the brake pedal, you

41
00:01:25,530 --> 00:01:27,850
react, which takes about one and a half

42
00:01:27,850 --> 00:01:30,090
seconds. During that time you're still

43
00:01:30,090 --> 00:01:32,330
moving at full speed, and that part only

44
00:01:32,330 --> 00:01:34,970
doubles. So the total stopping distance

45
00:01:34,970 --> 00:01:37,290
grows a bit less than four times, almost

46
00:01:37,290 --> 00:01:38,730
three times here.

47
00:01:38,730 --> 00:01:41,130
But the braking part, the part physics

48
00:01:41,130 --> 00:01:43,210
controls, quadruples.

49
00:01:43,210 --> 00:01:45,290
So remember this one line.

50
00:01:45,290 --> 00:01:47,360
Braking distance equals v squared over two

51
00:01:47,370 --> 00:01:50,010
a. Twice the speed, four times the braking

52
00:01:50,010 --> 00:01:50,710
distance.

53
00:01:50,920 --> 00:01:53,480
Next time: why highway curves are not

54
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:55,880
circles. See you there.
